 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T08:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**It depends on your health and how long you plan to be outside.** \n\nSingapore is experiencing significant haze, with the National Environment Agency (NEA) forecasting the 24-hour Pollutant Standards Index (PSI) to range between **150 and 230 (\"Unhealthy\" to \"Very Unhealthy\")**. \n\nWhile there are no legal mask mandates for being outdoors, the Ministry of Health (MOH) has issued specific advisories regarding mask usage for the haze:\n\n* **For short trips (commuting to work, school, or running a quick errand):** You generally **do not** need to wear a mask for brief outdoor exposure or while indoors.\n* **For prolonged outdoor exposure (several hours):** If you cannot avoid being outside for an extended period, you **should wear a mask**. \n  * **Healthy individuals** are advised to wear a mask if the 1-hour PM2.5 reading reaches **Band 4 (Very High)**. \n  * **Vulnerable individuals** (the elderly, pregnant women, and those with chronic heart or lung conditions) should wear one if they must go outside when the 1-hour PM2.5 reading is in **Band 3 (High)** or above. *(Note: Because N95 masks increase breathing resistance, vulnerable individuals with severe respiratory or heart issues should consult a doctor before using one, and it is best for them to simply stay indoors as much as possible).*\n\n**What type of mask to wear:**\nIf you do need to wear a mask for the haze, standard surgical or cloth masks will not filter out fine haze particles. MOH recommends wearing a well-fitted **N95, KN95, KF94, or FFP2 mask**, all of which are effective at reducing exposure to PM2.5 particles.\n\n**Best course of action:**\nBefore heading out, check the live 1-hour PM2.5 readings for your specific region on the [NEA website](https://www.haze.gov.sg/) or the myENV app. Even if you don't wear a mask, everyone is advised to minimize prolonged or strenuous outdoor physical exertion until the air quality improves.",
